# Copy to .env and edit. .env is git-ignored and must never be committed.
# Everything here is machine-specific; nothing here changes behaviour that code
# should own. Spoke and hub differ only through these values plus the matching
# Meltano environment and SQLMesh gateway.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# spoke (default) or hub. Selects the Meltano environment and, by default, the
# SQLMesh gateway of the same name. It never selects different code.
PLATFORM_NODE_ROLE=spoke

# Override only if you want a gateway whose name differs from the node role.
# SQLMESH_GATEWAY=spoke

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data plane
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the data plane lives. Relative paths resolve against the project root.
# PLATFORM_DATA_DIR=data

# local_only (default) keeps everything on this disk and makes `make sync` an
# explicit no-op. Set an s3:// URI to enable DVC-backed sync; `uv add dvc[s3]`
# is required before that will work.
DATA_REMOTE=local_only

# Credentials for the data remote. Required only when DATA_REMOTE is an s3 URI.
# Never commit real values.
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Official-source fetching (pipeline/fetch_law.py) -- OPERATOR TOOL, optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed if you use `python -m pipeline.fetch_law` to pull official docs
# into data/inbox/documents/. Both keys are free and neither is used by the
# build or the read path. Never commit real values.
#
# 법제처 국가법령정보 OPEN API (law.go.kr/DRF): register at open.law.go.kr
# (email verification only). OC is your registered id, not literally "test".
# LAW_OC=
#
# 국회 열린국회정보 OPEN API (open.assembly.go.kr): register and issue an 인증키.
# ASSEMBLY_API_KEY=
#
# DART 전자공시 OPEN API (opendart.fss.or.kr): register, a personal crtfc_key is
# issued instantly. Used by `fetch_law dart` (증권신고서, e.g. 투자계약증권/조각투자).
# DART_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retrieval
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hashing (default) is deterministic, local, and needs no model download, so the
# build never blocks on the network. It handles Korean via character n-grams:
# measured on Korean legal/government retrieval it lands close to a dense
# multilingual model ONCE FUSED with the keyword half, because statute queries
# reuse the statute's own vocabulary.
#
# What it cannot do is context or genuine synonymy: it is a bag of character
# n-grams, so word order and 조사/어미 disambiguation are invisible to it, and it
# will not match 원화 to 금전. For a CONTEXT-preserving upgrade, use the
# int8-quantised bge-m3 ONNX encoder: a real transformer (self-attention intact)
# that runs torch-free on CPU via onnxruntime. The model is ~568 MB, fetched once
# into the local cache, and query encoding is ~11 ms, so the read path stays
# offline and cheap:
#
#   uv sync --extra onnx-embed
#   EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=onnx_int8
#   EMBEDDING_MODEL=Xenova/bge-m3      # int8 ONNX; torch-free; ~568 MB, cached once
#
# Measured on the committed Korean bill eval (`make eval`), onnx_int8 vs hashing:
# the vector arm MRR@10 rises 0.469 -> 0.738 and the fused MRR@10 0.699 -> 0.744,
# with particle-glued queries reaching 1.000. It does NOT close the synonymy gap
# (0.444, unchanged) -- that is pipeline/aliases.tsv's job, not the embedder's.
# The index build re-encodes every chunk single-threaded for bit-stable rebuilds
# (~10 min for ~1200 chunks); the DEFAULT stays hashing so `make build` never
# needs a model. For any other Sentence-Transformers model (needs torch, ~2.2 GB):
#
#   uv add sentence-transformers
#   EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=sentence_transformers
#   EMBEDDING_MODEL=BAAI/bge-m3        # strong Korean; ~2.2 GB of weights + torch
#
# Changing any of these changes index_signature, so the next query fails loudly
# until you run `make build`. That is deliberate.
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=hashing
# 1024 is the measured knee for Korean. Below 512 the hashing provider refuses
# to start: Korean character n-grams collide badly in a small vector.
EMBEDDING_DIM=1024
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware: which asset, and therefore which execution provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# int8 (default) | fp16 | auto. This picks the ONNX ASSET, and the asset is what
# decides whether a GPU is usable at all:
#
#   int8  is a CPU format. On a GPU execution provider it needs Tensor-Core int8
#         plus a TensorRT calibration to pay off; without them it is SLOWER. That
#         is not a guess -- measured here on CoreML with the int8 bge-m3 graph:
#         the 1775-node graph split into 148 CoreML partitions and ran at 869
#         ms/chunk against 517 ms/chunk on a single CPU thread, with peak RSS
#         13.5 GB. So with int8, `pipeline/runtime.py` stays on the CPU even when
#         a GPU is present, on purpose.
#   fp16  is the GPU asset (~1.13 GB, fetched once). Selecting it lets runtime.py
#         use CUDA (Linux/Windows) or DirectML (any DX12 Windows GPU), whichever
#         this box registers. It is NOT free on a CPU: measured, a short QUERY
#         encodes in 12.7 ms with int8 and 203.4 ms with fp16 -- 16x, on the read
#         path, for identical vectors. (Bulk encoding of long chunks is a wash:
#         502 vs 466 ms/passage. The fp16 penalty is fixed overhead, so it hurts
#         short inputs most, and a query is the shortest input there is.)
#
# CoreML (macOS) is NOT selected automatically. Measured on this repo's models:
# int8 -> 148 partitions, 869 ms/chunk against 517 on one CPU thread, SIGKILL at
# 32 chunks; fp16 -> SIGSEGV on real chunk lengths. A native crash cannot be
# caught, so it is opt-in via ORT_PROVIDER=CoreMLExecutionProvider only.
#
# THIS IS A FLEET DECISION, NOT A PER-MACHINE ONE. The vectors in a synced index
# and the query encoder on every spoke have to come from the same asset, so the
# precision is part of index_signature: switch it, and every node rebuilds or
# fails loudly. `auto` (per-machine) is for a single-machine setup only.
#
# Before switching, measure YOUR box -- the numbers above are one machine's:
#   make gpu-probe     which provider is selected, and would fp16 change that
#   make bench-ep      ms/passage per (provider, asset), with the winner named
#   make warm-models   fetch the asset for the configured precision (once, online)
#   make eval          the retrieval floor for the new asset (record it, then gate)
# EMBEDDING_PRECISION=int8

# Which GPU stack is installed. The onnxruntime wheels (onnxruntime,
# onnxruntime-gpu, onnxruntime-directml) are THE SAME MODULE under different
# names and must never be installed together -- so this is `make setup-gpu
# GPU=cuda` or `GPU=directml`, never both, and macOS needs neither (CoreML ships
# in the default wheel). `make gpu-probe` reports which wheel is installed and
# what it actually registered.
#
# DEVICE=auto|cpu|gpu   policy override. cpu pins everything to the CPU.
# ORT_PROVIDER=<EPName> force one provider; fails loudly if it is not registered.
# ENCODE_WORKERS=<n>    parallel CPU sessions (default: cores-2, capped at 8).
#
# DEVICE_PLACEMENT="embedder=gpu,reranker=npu,ocr=cpu" -- pin a STAGE to a device
# CLASS (cpu|gpu|npu), never to a provider name, so one setting keeps working
# across machines with different vendors. Selection is by class throughout: the
# asset follows the class (cpu int8 / gpu fp16 / NPU int8 -- an NPU is an integer
# engine, so the GPU rule is wrong for it), and a stage pinned to a class with no
# eligible device falls back to the CPU with a stated reason rather than failing.
# `make gpu-probe` lists every device with its class, which enumeration reported
# it, and why it is or is not eligible.
# DEVICE_PLACEMENT=
#
# RESIDENT_BUDGET_MB -- warn when this much model weight is loaded at once
# (default: 40% of physical RAM). Sessions are cached for the life of the
# process, so a run can hold the embedder AND the reranker; measured, those two
# alone peaked at 4.47 GB RSS against ~1.1 GB of weights. This warns, it does not
# evict: at most two models are resident today, and a cache that silently dropped
# a session would trade a memory warning for a latency mystery.
# RESIDENT_BUDGET_MB=
#
# ENCODE_BATCH=<n> -- passages per forward pass, 16 everywhere. This looks like a
# throughput knob and is NOT one: the tokenizer pads to the longest sequence in
# the batch, so the batch's composition changes the numbers the graph produces
# (measured: cosine 0.9918 between one passage encoded alone and the same passage
# batched beside a long one -- three orders of magnitude larger than any
# execution-provider difference). It is therefore part of the VECTOR definition:
# index_signature records any override, so a fleet cannot half-adopt one, and the
# GPU deliberately does not get a bigger batch than the CPU.
# ENCODE_BATCH=16
#
# ENCODE_DEVICES=auto|hybrid -- `hybrid` (Tier B) also gives the CPU a share of
# the batches while the GPU runs. It is OFF by default and worth it only where
# the GPU is under ~3x the CPU (integrated graphics), because both devices must
# run the SAME asset: the CPU half would run the fp16 graph through inserted
# casts, well below its native int8 speed. `make bench-ep` prints the ratio and
# says whether hybrid is worth turning on. The split is measured once and RECORDED
# in data/processed/device_profile.json so a rerun reproduces the same assignment
# (ENCODE_SPLIT=g:c pins it by hand and skips the measurement).
# ENCODE_DEVICES=auto

# The index build reuses vectors for passages whose text has not changed
# (data/processed/vector_cache.sqlite, keyed by the hash of the encoded text and
# the vector signature). It is why landing one document no longer re-encodes the
# corpus. EMBED_CACHE=0 forces a full re-encode; `make clean` drops the cache,
# `make clean-index` deliberately keeps it.
# EMBED_CACHE=1

# Reciprocal rank fusion constant. Larger values flatten the contribution of top
# ranks, which helps when one retrieval half is much noisier than the other.
RRF_K=60

# The two arms are not equally trustworthy on Korean: the keyword half matches
# statute vocabulary exactly, the vector half is a bag of character n-grams.
# Measured on 45 Korean queries, weighting the vector arm at 0.3 lifts MRR@10
# from 0.706 to 0.725 and takes particle-glued queries to 1.000. Flat across
# 0.3-0.5, so there is no knife-edge. Do NOT set it to 0: dropping the arm
# breaks exact-identifier retrieval.
VECTOR_WEIGHT=0.3
KEYWORD_WEIGHT=1.0

# Query-side domain synonym expansion (pipeline/aliases.tsv). Bridges
# 스테이블코인 to 자산연동형, which share no characters and are therefore
# invisible to both halves of the index. Query-time only: turning it off needs
# no rebuild. Measured MRR@10 0.725 -> 0.802, at 8.7 ms/query against 4.5 ms.
ALIAS_EXPANSION=1

# --- Optional cross-encoder reranker (OPT-IN, default OFF) -------------------
# The baseline (no reranker) is the M1-8GB target. Turning this on loads a
# SECOND ~544 MB model (bge-reranker-v2-m3 int8). MEASURED peak RSS (ru_maxrss /
# `time -l`) is workload-dependent and RISES with candidates and query volume:
# ~1.7 GB for a few queries at candidates=20, ~2.5 GB for 2 and ~3.4 GB for 5
# queries at candidates=40. So ~3.5 GB is realistic for sustained candidates=40
# use (comfortable on 8 GB, not featherweight); the default candidates=20 is
# lighter. Point-in-time RSS undercounts (onnx mmap) -- trust a peak for YOUR
# workload, and note peak keeps climbing over queries (worth confirming it
# plateaus for a long-lived server).
#
# THOSE FIGURES ASSUME THE DEFAULT `EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=hashing`, which loads no
# embedding model at all. They do NOT carry over to the onnx_int8 option offered
# above -- that provider is itself resident. Measured on one machine
# (2026-07-27, candidates=20): a single query costs 2.50 GB peak with the
# reranker OFF, and 4.47 GB with it ON. Budget for the SUM of the two models,
# not for the reranker alone, if you enable both on an 8 GB box.
#
# LATENCY is the reason this stays off, and it is the cost the retrieval eval
# cannot see (it scores ranking, not wall-clock). Measured on one machine
# (2026-07-27, candidates=20, EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=onnx_int8): `make query`
# end-to-end goes 2.24 s -> 32.68 s, a 14.6x slowdown. Model load is only 0.7 s
# and happens once, so the cost is INFERENCE -- a long-lived server does not
# amortise it away; it pays 11-30 s on every query. "Query-time only, no
# rebuild" means cheap to TURN ON, not cheap to RUN.
#
# So prefer opting in per invocation, where the extra seconds buy something and
# nobody is waiting: `RERANK=1 make ask Q="..."`.
#
# The read path never downloads: run `make warm-rerank` once online first, then
# gate any change with `make eval-rerank`.
#   RERANK=1
# RERANK_MODEL=onnx-community/bge-reranker-v2-m3-ONNX
# Candidates reranked per query. On this corpus 20 is the MEASURED floor: it
# reproduces 40's every per-kind metric at ~2x the speed, while 15 drops
# cross_bill and 10 drops vocab too (the relevant chunk falls outside the pool).
# Raise only with CPU/RAM headroom; K>100 was measured to hurt on larger sets.
# RERANK_CANDIDATES=20
# Retrieval PRIOR weight in the fusion (CE weight is fixed at 1.0): lower = more
# cross-encoder-dominant. Alias chunks are protected by variant max-pool, so the
# prior only breaks near-ties. Tune via `make eval-rerank`.
# RERANK_WEIGHT=0.15
# ONNX threads. 1 keeps the ranking deterministic (default); raise for speed on
# a machine with headroom, accepting tiny run-to-run float wobble. MEASURED here:
# 520 ms/pair at 1 thread, 259 ms at 8 -- roughly 2x, and sub-linear because int8
# GEMM is memory-bandwidth-bound. This is the cheapest thing to try before any GPU.
# RERANK_THREADS=1
# int8 (default) | fp16 | auto -- the reranker's asset, same rule as
# EMBEDDING_PRECISION: fp16 is what lets runtime.py put it on a GPU. UNLIKE the
# embedder this is machine-local, because no reranker output is stored or synced;
# it only has to agree with the recorded eval-rerank floor, so re-record with
# `make eval-rerank-baseline` after changing it.
# RERANK_PRECISION=int8
